In article <GWILD.91Oct11110709@navier.ae.utexas.edu> gwild@navier.ae.utexas.edu (Gregory Wildgrube) writes:
>>Don't stay too long on a level. When you have investigated all rooms, there
>>is no reason to stay at that level. Also an other problems occurs when you
>>play to slow. The difficulty of the game increases more than you can find
>>good objects.
>Well...if you keep on descending levels without allowing you character
>to advance in levels, you will die quickly as well...I like stay on
>the first level until I reach exp. level 4-5.
And? Do you win?

The difficulty of the game depends on the dungeon level and your experience
level. I don't know which one is more important.

I saw once somebody using a trick with a ring of levitation and a sink. He
killed many puddings and got a relative high experience level but he died
almost immediate after he left the room with the sink.

So, my policy is, go to the next level when you investigated all rooms and
walk efficient. If you wait until you reach experience level 4-5 on the first
level, you will probably get a bunch of orcs on dungeon level 3 and a shop
on level 4 which you will never reach.

I won several games by playing fast (but properly) the first 15 levels.

